00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:14.250
me to drive because I was, like, I'm going to be in L.A.
教我开车，因为我想，我要去洛杉矶了。

323
00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:15.810
You have to teach me to drive.
你必须教我开车。

324
00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:19.890
So my dad taught me to drive, like, some weird, like, little short film or something.
所以我爸教我开车，就像拍什么奇怪的短片之类的。

325
00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:25.810
Like, and then I remember when we started shooting, driving terrified me so much that
然后我记得刚开始拍摄时，开车让我非常害怕，以至于

326
00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:31.590
I would show up on set and my jaw would be so clenched that I had a hard time saying words.
我到片场时，下巴紧绷到连话都说不清楚。

327
00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:38.010
So I would take CDs, Frank Sinatra CDs, and I would put them in the car and force myself
所以我带了CD，弗兰克·辛纳屈的CD，我在车里放这些歌，强迫自己

328
00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:43.070
to sing along with old blue eyes so that my jaw would stay open so that I wouldn't show
跟着这位“老蓝眼”一起唱，好让我的下巴保持放松，这样我就不会

329
00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:45.510
up with, like, rigor mortis face.
带着一副僵尸脸出现在片场。

330
00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:52.470
And then I think before I'd even shot my first scene, being in a Walmart, buying, like, multiple
然后我想在拍第一场戏之前，我在沃尔玛，买了

331
00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:57.830
humidifiers and lozenges and shit because I was like, I'm going to lose my voice and then
好几个加湿器、润喉糖之类的东西，因为我担心我会失声，然后

332
00:12:57.830 --> 00:12:59.090
I'm not going to be able to do it.
我就没法演了。

333
00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.690
And I'm going to, I was so panicked and I was worried I was going to forget my lines.
我当时非常恐慌，担心自己会忘词。

334
00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:04.870
So I was, like, muttering my lines in a Walmart.
所以我当时在沃尔玛里嘟囔着台词。

335
00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:06.370
For some reason, I didn't get a basket.
不知为什么，我没拿篮子。

336
00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:11.630
So I just had, like, armfuls of, like, humidifiers and lozenges just, like, muttering, like, my
所以我怀里抱着一大堆加湿器和润喉糖，像个疯子一样

337
00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:13.550
lines to myself like a crazy person.
自言自语地嘟囔着台词。

338
00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:19.050
So what I really remember from that time is just the white hot panic of, like, you know,
所以我对那段时光记忆最深的就是那种极度的恐慌，你知道的，

339
00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:19.270
like.
那种感觉。

340
00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:21.510
Were you prone to losing your voice?
你以前容易失声吗？

341
00:13:21.770 --> 00:13:24.510
What was this anxiety about losing your voice?
为什么会担心失声这种焦虑？

342
00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:25.590
I was prone to losing my mind.
我容易失去理智。

343
00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:26.170
I was like.
我当时就是这样。

344
00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:27.170
Put you in the Walmart.
把你放在沃尔玛里。

345
00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:29.150
I think it was just, like, anything.
我想那只是，随便什么事吧。

346
00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:30.630
You know, you have, like, free ranging.
你知道，你会有那种，漫无目的的焦虑。

347
00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:34.450
Sometimes I feel like anxiety is just, like, a giant, like, military helicopter that needs
有时我觉得焦虑就像一架巨大的军用直升机，需要

348
00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:35.490
some place to land.
找个地方降落。

349
00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:40.290
And it was like, oh, today it'll be landing on the landing pad of, like, vocal anxiety.
当时的情况就是，哦，今天它要降落在“嗓音焦虑”的停机坪上。

350
00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:44.850
Tomorrow it'll be, like, whatever that I'll, you know, some weird hypochondriacal thing
明天它可能又会降落在，随便什么奇怪的疑病症上。

351
00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:45.750
in a different direction.
朝着不同的方向。

352
00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:46.230
Yes.
是的。

353
00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:46.610
Yes.
是的。

354
00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:50.910
So I don't remember what they told me, but I didn't think I was, I thought it might just
我不记得他们当时是怎么跟我说的了，但我没觉得自己会留下，我以为可能只是

355
00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:54.750
be a few episodes and then they gave me more and I was like, wow, that's great.
拍几集而已，结果他们又给了我更多戏份，我当时就想，哇，那太棒了。

356
00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:56.890
Do you remember your first day on set?
你还记得在片场的第一天吗？

357
00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:57.610
Yeah.
记得。

358
00:13:57.930 --> 00:13:59.990
What did you shoot your very first day?
你第一天拍的是什么？

359
00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:03.750
I think the first thing I shot was a, to camera, like, one of the interviews.
我想我拍的第一件事是对着镜头，就是那种采访。

360
00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:04.630
A talking head.
“谈话头”采访。

361
00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:05.190
Talking head.
对，谈话头。

362
00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:05.490
Okay.
好的。

363
00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:09.390
That's a good way to start, actually, because it's just you.
其实这是个很好的开始方式，因为只有你一个人。

364
00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:12.010
No one is dependent on your line for timing.
没有人会因为你的台词而影响节奏。

365
00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:13.430
You're not driving a scene.
你不需要主导整场戏。

366
00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:13.750
Right.
没错。

367
00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:16.010
So I think that's actually a really smart way to start.
所以我认为这确实是一个非常聪明的开始方式。

368
00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:17.650
They might have done that on purpose, too.
他们可能也是故意这么安排的。

369
00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:20.230
What was your first impression coming on the set?
你刚到片场时的第一印象是什么？

370
00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:23.690
Well, John Krasinski directed the episode that I was doing.
嗯，我拍的那集是约翰·卡拉辛斯基执导的。

371
00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:28.250
And I remember something happened on set and it, like, took longer than they were expecting.
我记得片场出了点状况，比他们预期的花的时间要长。

372
00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:30.090
So I was sort of in my trailer just churning.
所以我当时就在拖车里有点坐立难安。

373
00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:31.810
And then they called me to set.
然后他们叫我去片场。

374
00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:34.370
And I think John knew that I was kind of, like, pent up and terrified.
我想约翰看出了我当时有点紧张和害怕。

375
00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:42.570
And so he just, like, even though I'm sure it had been a long day and there was a lot left to shoot, he, like, let me just improvise a lot in the talking head.
所以他，尽管我肯定那天已经很漫长了，还有很多戏要拍，但他还是让我对着镜头即兴发挥了很多。

376
00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:45.910
And I doubt they used any of it, but it just was, like, it let me relax.
我怀疑他们最后根本没用上，但这确实让我放松了下来。

377
00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.170
And he was so kind about it.
他当时真的非常体贴。

378
00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:56.110
But the big thing that I remember, and part of why I was, like, excited to do this is because, like, I'm not good at staying in touch with people and stuff.
但我记得最深刻的一件事，也是我当初很兴奋能参与其中的原因，就是我不太擅长和人保持联系之类的。

379
00:14:56.250 --> 00:15:02.510
But I feel, yeah, that I feel like is important to me to communicate to people who listen to this,
但我确实觉得，是的，我觉得向听众传达这一点对我来说很重要，

380
00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:10.790
which is the level of kindness and hospitality that was extended to me on that show was bizarre.
那就是我在那个剧组感受到的那种善意和好客程度简直不可思议。

381
00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:21.090
Like, if you're in the late seasons of a TV show that has, like, such a deep bench of amazing characters and some new person shows up who's going to suck up even more oxygen,
你想想，如果一部电视剧已经拍到了后期，拥有那么多出色的角色，突然来了一个新人，还要占用更多的资源，

382
00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:23.390
eat up more story time, you know what I mean?
抢占更多的剧情时间，你懂我的意思吗？

383
00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:31.430
And it would be very easy to understand if people were not all that receptive to that, especially if it's someone who's, like, a rookie who's still just, like, learning about,
如果大家对新人没那么热情，尤其是像我这种还在学习的新手，那完全是可以理解的，

384
00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:33.230
like, okay, what's an eyeline?
比如，什么是视线？

385
00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.150
How do I, like, what's a mark?
我该怎么做，什么是机位标记？

386
00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:35.750
You know what I mean?
你懂我意思吗？

387
00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:40.010
Like, stuff, like, just very rookie stuff that I'm having to learn on the go.
就是那些我必须边拍边学的非常基础的东西。

388
00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:46.590
Like, it would be totally reasonable if people had a kind of professional detachment from that person.
如果大家对那个人保持一种职业上的疏离感，那也是完全合理的。

389
00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:50.810
But everyone was so ostentatiously kind to me.
但每个人都对我表现得特别友善。

390
00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:52.110
Ostentatious makes it sound bad.
用“特别”这个词听起来好像有点贬义。

391
00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:54.110
It just generous, kind.
应该说是慷慨、友善。

392
00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:56.470
Like, you guys were so lovely to me.
你们对我真的太好了。

393
00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:58.550
You guys were so sweet to me and inclusive.
你们对我非常贴心，也很包容。

394
00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:07.790
I remember, like, I would just sort of camp out at, like, Phyllis and Leslie's desk and watch them, like, shop for – they were both, like, decorating their houses and they were,
我记得我经常会待在菲利斯和莱斯利的办公桌旁，看她们购物——她们当时都在装饰自己的房子，然后她们，

395
00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:08.310
like –
就像是——

396
00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:11.570
Yes, she was looking for a gate for, like, I think two years.
是的，她找那个大门，找了大概有两年吧。

397
00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:12.730
She wanted a new –
她想要一个新的——

398
00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:12.890
More, maybe.
可能还不止两年。

399
00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:13.250
Yeah.
是的。

400
00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:15.410
We looked at every gate possible.
我们把所有可能的大门都看遍了。

401
00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:21.990
I just remember basically, yeah, like, gate shopping with Leslie and Phyllis and, like, they would show me and they would be so nice and joke around with me.
我基本上只记得，对，就像，和莱斯利还有菲利斯一起去逛街，她们会带我到处看看，人特别好，还会跟我开玩笑。

402
00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:24.790
I remember Kate Flannery really went out of her way.
我记得凯特·弗兰纳里真的特别照顾我。

403
00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:25.670
Oscar.
奥斯卡。

404
00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:29.590
And not just in a kind – again, not just in a perfunctory, like, professional way.
而且不仅仅是那种——再说一次，不仅仅是那种敷衍的、职业化的客套。

405
00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:31.290
They'd invite me to their houses.
她们会邀请我去她们家里。

406
00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:33.730
You guys would include me in, like, social stuff.
你们会带我参加各种社交活动。

407
00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:39.110
I just – it was so – it was like the photo negative of what happened to the character Gabe, right?
我只是觉得——这太——这简直就是盖博这个角色遭遇的底片，对吧？

408
00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:45.130
Like, Gabe is, like, this, like, unfortunate creepy guy shows up and is immediately and utterly ostracized.
就像，盖博是个不幸的怪胎，一出现就立刻被彻底排挤了。

409
00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:49.430
It was, like – yeah, it was the exact opposite where I was, like, oh, my God, look at this.
而我这边——对，完全是相反的，我当时就想，天哪，看看这个。

410
00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:52.370
Like, this group of people is just, like, so envelopingly sweet.
这群人简直太包容、太贴心了。

411
00:16:52.590 --> 00:17:01.970
So that was life-changing because I was already so fragile and terrified because it was such a new experience and I wanted so desperately to do a good job.
所以那段经历改变了我的人生，因为我当时已经非常脆弱和恐惧了，毕竟那是一个全新的体验，我非常渴望能表现好。

412
00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:09.670
And to be met with that kind of generosity of spirit was, I think, formative for me.
能遇到那种慷慨的精神支持，我认为对我来说具有塑造性的意义。

413
00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:11.670
I mean, we loved the character Gabe.
我是说，我们很喜欢盖博这个角色。

414
00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:16.030
I mean, in re-watching, like, Gabe has made us laugh so hard.
我是说，重温的时候，盖博真的让我们笑得好开心。

415
00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:25.210
But one of the things we've been most excited about doing this podcast is being able to reconnect with these people that were such an amazing chapter of our life.
但我们做这个播客最兴奋的事情之一，就是能和这些曾经是我们生命中精彩篇章的人重新建立联系。

416
00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:27.710
And you're one of those people we've talked about you so much.
而你就是其中之一，我们谈论过你很多次。

417
00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:29.130
We're so happy you're here.
我们很高兴你能来。

418
00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:39.530
I mean, we still talked about the gift you brought to my Yankee Swap Christmas party, which was a sarcophagus, like, jewelry box.
我是说，我们还在谈论你带到我那场“洋基交换”圣诞派对上的礼物，那是一个石棺造型的珠宝盒。

419
00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:43.510
It was the gift that everyone wanted.
那是每个人都想要的礼物。

420
00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:46.030
I mean, legend gift.
我是说，传奇礼物。

421
00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:46.830
Thank you.
谢谢。

422
00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:51.270
I mean, that's – like, white elephant stuff is, like, a real source of anxiety, right?
我是说，那种——白象交换礼物真的让人很焦虑，对吧？

423
00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:54.650
Because it's, like – it feels like a moral, spiritual teSt.
因为它感觉就像一场道德和精神上的考验。

424
00:17:55.210 --> 00:17:56.470
So, I'm glad that I passed.
所以我很高兴我通过了考验。

425
00:17:57.010 --> 00:17:59.750
You passed, and I have the sarcophagus.
你通过了，而我拥有了那个石棺。

426
00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:00.390
You do?
你还有吗？

427
00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:01.510
Yes, it's mine.
是的，它是我的。

428
00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:03.190
Yeah, I went home with it.
对，我把它带回家了。

429
00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:04.770
Yeah, I have it on my bookshelf.
没错，它现在就在我的书架上。

430
00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:06.050
And it's not cursed or anything, right?
它没被诅咒什么的吧？

431
00:18:06.310 --> 00:18:07.290
Doesn't seem like it.
看起来没有。

432
00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:07.970
Okay, cool.
好的，太棒了。

433
00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:09.430
Yeah, it seems like a dead you.
是啊，看起来像是死去的你。

434
00:18:09.590 --> 00:18:16.110
I hadn't actively pursued a curse, but if there's a small sarcophagus, the odds that it has some sort of curse feels pretty high.
我并没有刻意去寻找诅咒，但如果有一个小石棺，它带有某种诅咒的几率感觉还是挺高的。

435
00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:17.910
It's been good so far.
目前为止还不错。

436
00:18:18.190 --> 00:18:19.450
It's doing great for you.
它对你来说挺好的。

437
00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:19.950
It is.
确实。

438
00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:22.050
It was a heated battle that you won.
那是一场你赢了的激烈争夺战。

439
00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:29.070
And I also – off topic here, but in anticipation of talking to you today, I sort of went back through old emails.
还有——虽然离题了，但为了今天和你聊天，我特意翻了翻旧邮件。

440
00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:35.830
And I have some great photos of you that I sent a long, long time ago to you to probably an old email.
我有一些你很棒的照片，是很久很久以前发给你的，可能发到了你的旧邮箱里。

441
00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:36.470
But they're great.
但照片真的很棒。

442
00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:38.050
They're just of you hanging out on set.
就是你平时在片场休息时的样子。

443
00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:40.650
So, I need to make sure you get those.
所以我得确保你能收到那些照片。

444
00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:41.130
Oh, I would love that.
哦，我太想要了。

445
00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:41.390
Yeah.
好。

446
00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:42.110
I would love that.
我太想要了。

447
00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:42.450
Yeah.
好。

448
00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:44.850
Not that I was a creeper taking pictures of you.
并不是说我是个偷拍你的变态。

449
00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:50.030
There's like my camera in the men's room, which I got a lot of candid snaps.
就像我在男厕所装了摄像头，拍到了很多抓拍照片一样。

450
00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:50.670
No.
不。

451
00:18:50.890 --> 00:18:51.430
But I did.
但我确实做了。

452
00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:53.450
I found a few when we were at Shroot FarMs.
我们在什鲁特农场的时候我发现了一些。

453
00:18:53.570 --> 00:18:53.670
Oh, yeah.
噢，是啊。

454
00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:54.470
And they're just great.
而且它们棒极了。

455
00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:56.130
So, I'm going to make sure you get those.
所以我一定要确保你能拿到那些。

456
00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:56.910
With Groban.
和格罗班一起。

457
00:18:57.770 --> 00:18:58.830
Yes, with Josh.
是的，和乔什一起。

458
00:18:59.090 --> 00:18:59.350
Josh Groban.
乔什·格罗班。

459
00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:01.790
That's a really surreal moment.
那真是个非常超现实的时刻。

460
00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:07.050
I'd be like, I'm on a farm with Josh Groban and like all these people have been watching for years.
我会觉得，我竟然在农场里和乔什·格罗班在一起，还有这些大家看了好多年的人。

461
00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:08.250
It's just such a weird.
这真是太奇怪了。

462
00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:09.070
Where am I?
我在哪儿？

463
00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:09.270
Yeah.
是啊。

464
00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:11.010
Did someone like dose me with acid?
是不是有人给我下了迷幻药？

465
00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:16.810
And I'm like just back in my basement apartment in Hell's Kitchen having like a weird fever dream.
我就像回到了地狱厨房的地下室公寓里，做着一场奇怪的白日梦。

466
00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:17.650
Yeah.
是啊。

467
00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:25.150
So, I watched like your first couple of episodes, which must have been so insane because you're talking about being new and everything.
所以我看了你最初的那几集，那一定很疯狂，因为你当时还在谈论作为新人的感受之类的。

468
00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:25.650
Right.
没错。

469
00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:33.630
So, like there's the scene where you enter Dunder Mifflin for the first time and Dwight is holding a giant tray of hot dogs.
比如你第一次走进邓德·米夫林公司，德怀特端着一大盘热狗的那场戏。

470
00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:38.830
And then Aaron and Andy sing the Sabre song.
然后亚伦和安迪唱了赛博公司的歌。

471
00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:39.530
Yes.
是的。

472
00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:41.070
That is so crazy.
那真是太疯狂了。

473
00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:45.990
And then the next episode, you're like wrestling some Great Danes and you're talking to Kathy Bates.
接着下一集，你就在和几只大丹犬摔跤，还在和凯西·贝茨说话。

474
00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:47.030
Kathy Bates.
凯西·贝茨。

475
00:19:47.210 --> 00:19:47.430
I know.
我知道。

476
00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:47.890
That's crazy.
太疯狂了。

477
00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:48.180
Just right out.
简直不可思议。

478
00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:51.430
When did they tell you that you would be working with Kathy Bates?
他们是什么时候告诉你将要和凯西·贝茨合作的？

479
00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:54.030
You know, this is not a satisfying answer.
你知道，这个答案可能没法让你满意。

480
00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:57.030
But the truth is, again, it's just this sort of like pummeling.
但事实是，那又是一阵狂轰滥炸。

481
00:19:58.030 --> 00:19:58.750
Of anxiety.
焦虑的狂轰滥炸。

482
00:19:58.930 --> 00:19:59.110
Yeah.
是啊。

483
00:19:59.110 --> 00:19:59.910
Like I don't even know.
我甚至都不知道。

484
00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:02.850
I felt like I was like being beaten into a happy gang or something.
我感觉自己就像是被强行塞进了一个快乐的团队里一样。

485
00:20:02.870 --> 00:20:07.070
It was like just like it's just like, I don't know, like Kathy Bates, like the office,
就像是，我不知道，就像凯西·贝茨，就像办公室，

486
00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:13.690
like you're driving and you're going to be like it just felt like this kind of like word jumble of like, I don't know.
就像你在开车，然后你就要去面对这些，感觉就像是一堆乱七八糟的词汇，我不知道。

487
00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:14.190
What's next?
接下来是什么？

488
00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:14.590
Yeah.
是啊。

489
00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:17.730
I just felt like like I don't remember finding it out.
我只觉得，我不记得是怎么知道这件事的了。

490
00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:19.890
Like it's like the fog of war.
就像战争迷雾一样。

491
00:20:20.230 --> 00:20:21.530
I'm just like I don't remember.
我只是觉得我不记得了。

492
00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:22.470
Like.
就像。

493
00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:23.930
Like that's very funny.
那太搞笑了。

494
00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:27.190
We were so nervous about Kathy Bates being on set.
我们当时对凯西·贝茨来到片场感到非常紧张。

495
00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:27.610
Really?
真的吗？

496
00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:28.010
Gosh.
天哪。

497
00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:28.270
Yeah.
是啊。

498
00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:29.230
What were you nervous about?
你们在紧张什么？

499
00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:30.250
Just that she would tell me.
就是怕她会对我指指点点。

500
00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:31.030
Just anything.
就是任何方面。

501
00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:32.950
Just like making eye contact.
就像眼神交流一样。

502
00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:35.370
And she was such a presence.
而且她气场十足。

503
00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:36.950
And then she was so kind.
但她人又特别好。

504
00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:40.810
I think it was just her stature and her body of work.
我想这主要归功于她的地位和她的代表作。

505
00:20:40.810 --> 00:20:47.190
And we had avoided for so long having like these big name actors on the show.
我们一直以来都尽量避免邀请这种大牌演员上节目。

506
00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:51.730
And so this was kind of a turning point and it was very shocking.
所以这算是一个转折点，而且非常令人震惊。

507
00:20:51.730 --> 00:20:53.250
It felt like, you know.
那种感觉，你知道的。

508
00:20:53.390 --> 00:20:53.630
Yeah.
是啊。

509
00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:59.050
So my hair colorist had once colored Kathy Bates' hair.
我的发型师曾经给凯西·贝茨染过头发。

510
00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:08.130
And he told me this like years ago, you know, and just, you know, when hairdressers just tell you their lore while they're doing your hair.
他几年前告诉过我，你知道的，就是那种发型师在给你做头发时会跟你讲的那些趣事。

511
00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:10.870
And he's like, well, you know, I used to color Kathy Bates' hair.
他说，嗯，你知道吗，我以前给凯西·贝茨染过头发。

512
00:21:10.870 --> 00:21:12.690
And, oh, what a woman.
天哪，那真是位了不起的女性。

513
00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:15.110
And he just like so admired her and all this.
他当时非常崇拜她，诸如此类的。

514
00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:18.650
He didn't gossip, but he just did name drop that he had colored her hair.
他没有八卦，只是顺口提了一句他给凯西·贝茨染过发。

515
00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:31.310
So when I knew she was coming on the show, I had to resist that urge, you know, when like the second I saw her not to be like, we have the same hair coloriSt.
所以当我知道她要来节目时，我必须克制住那种冲动，你知道的，就是见到她的那一刻，不要脱口而出：我们用的是同一个发型师。

516
00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:31.850
Hi.
你好。

517
00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:34.510
I'm Jenna, our hair coloriSt.
我是珍娜，我们的发型师。

518
00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:36.650
He colored your hair once.
他曾经给你染过头发。

519
00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:38.850
Welcome to the show.
欢迎来到节目。

520
00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:43.430
I'm so impressed that you suppressed that because I know that would come just tumbling out of my mouth.
我很佩服你能忍住没说，因为我知道换做是我，肯定会脱口而出。

521
00:21:43.550 --> 00:21:44.370
I held it.
我忍住了。

522
00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:45.790
I can't remember for how long.
我不记得忍了多久。

523
00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:48.430
I think I made it several days.
我想我坚持了好几天。

524
00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:55.030
And then I did finally say, you know, Robert Hickland hair coloriSt.
最后我还是说了，你知道的，罗伯特·希克兰，发型师。

525
00:21:55.250 --> 00:21:59.070
I think we, I think he, oh, yes, Bobby.
我想我们，我想他，哦，是的，鲍比。

526
00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:00.130
Yes, I know him.
是的，我认识他。

527
00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:01.170
Yes, wonderful guy.
没错，是个很棒的人。

528
00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:02.550
We should be best friends now.
我们现在应该是好朋友了。

529
00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:04.310
Well, that was my next line.
嗯，那本来是我想说的下一句。

530
00:22:04.610 --> 00:22:06.590
So we are clearly best friends then.
所以我们显然是好朋友了。

531
00:22:06.830 --> 00:22:07.870
Same hair coloriSt.
同一个发型师。

532
00:22:07.870 --> 00:22:08.950
So much in common.
我们有这么多共同点。

533
00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:10.530
I'm your emergency contact.
我是你的紧急联系人。

534
00:22:10.690 --> 00:22:11.390
Yeah, exactly.
是啊，没错。

535
00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:14.890
Let me ask you, so because for many people, you are that.
我想问问，因为对很多人来说，你就是那样的存在。

536
00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:20.910
Like, in other words, when people see you, right, it generates the same feelings in them that Kathy Bage generated in you.
换句话说，当人们看到你时，这会激起他们和你见到凯西·贝茨时一样的感受。

537
00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:27.390
So what is the version, like when people come up to you guys and are like, actually, did you know that we shared a, you know, whatever?
那么，当人们走过来对你们说，其实你知道吗，我们有个什么共同点的时候，你们是什么感觉？

538
00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:29.770
We stay in the same summer camp cabin?
比如，我们住过同一个夏令营小屋？

539
00:22:30.310 --> 00:22:33.410
Like, does it feel alienating to you when people do that?
当人们这样做时，你会觉得疏远吗？

540
00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:34.270
Or does it feel nice?
还是觉得挺好的？

541
00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:35.470
Or does it just depend on the person?
还是说这取决于对方是谁？

542
00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:38.510
Like, well, I guess what I'm asking is like, or maybe is it okay to ask you guys?
我想我真正想问的是，或者说，问你们这些问题可以吗？

543
00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:38.730
Yeah.
可以。

544
00:22:38.890 --> 00:22:39.330
Yes, please.
是的，请问吧。

545
00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:51.330
What is the version of being approached in a situation like that where maybe it's not just like in the airport, but it's like a situation that has some containment.
在那种情况下，比如不是在机场那种场合，而是在一个相对封闭的环境里，什么样的搭讪方式让你觉得舒服？

546
00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:52.070
Yeah.
嗯。

547
00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:58.450
That feels good to you versus the one that feels like, like if you're Kathy Bates, how do you want someone to approach you?
什么样的让你感觉良好，而什么样的又让你觉得不适？如果你是凯西·贝茨，你希望别人怎么接近你？

548
00:22:58.630 --> 00:23:02.790
Well, I'll say that I just did the Mean Girls movie musical.
嗯，我刚拍完《贱女孩》的音乐电影。

549
00:23:02.930 --> 00:23:03.170
Yeah.
是啊。

550
00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:04.210
I play the mom.
我在里面演妈妈。

551
00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:04.690
Yay!
耶！

552
00:23:04.690 --> 00:23:21.650
And after I got on the set and I like was working, I called Angela and I was like, Angela, we are now like the old lady, like the people, like these kids and the crew and stuff.
当我到了片场开始工作时，我给安吉拉打了电话，我说，安吉拉，我们现在就像当年的那些老前辈一样，就像那些孩子和剧组人员眼里的老前辈。

553
00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:31.830
They're treating me like the way we were around like Kathy Bates or around some of these like people who had like the resumes and all that kind of stuff.
他们对待我的方式，就像我们当年对待凯西·贝茨或者其他那些资历深厚的前辈一样。

554
00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:33.350
I'm like, it's so weird.
我心想，这太奇怪了。

555
00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:43.450
And it feels like it was so bizarre because I was like, oh, I'm like not at all worthy of your, what do you call it?
这种感觉真的很离奇，因为我当时想，噢，我根本配不上你们的……怎么说呢？

556
00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:50.610
Like they're just giving me like, like, yes, like sort of like, we thank you so much for doing our film.
他们就像是在对我说，是的，非常感谢你来拍我们的电影。

557
00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:53.330
And the directors were just like, we can't believe we got you.
导演们也说，真不敢相信我们竟然请到了你。

558
00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:54.730
And I'm like, you can't?
我心想，真的吗？

559
00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:57.410
I like, it's the Mean Girls movie musical.
我心想，这可是《贱女孩》的音乐剧电影啊。

560
00:23:57.590 --> 00:23:59.530
I feel like people are lining up to do this.
我觉得大家应该都排着队想来演吧。

561
00:23:59.530 --> 00:24:02.490
You're being so overly kind to me.
你们对我太客气了。

562
00:24:02.590 --> 00:24:09.310
But I do remember that, like when we would be at an award show, like someone from a television show that we had watched,
但我确实记得，当我们参加颁奖典礼时，如果遇到我们平时看过的电视剧里的演员，

563
00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:13.490
you get a chance to meet them or they give you an award or something.
你有机会见到他们，或者他们给你颁奖之类的，

564
00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:16.050
You're like, oh, my God, I can't believe this person.
你会觉得，天哪，真不敢相信是这个人。

565
00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:18.450
I was like, we're the old timers now.
我当时就想，我们现在成了老前辈了。

566
00:24:18.670 --> 00:24:18.970
Yeah.
是啊。

567
00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:21.670
We're the old timers who have been in the biz for a while.
我们成了在这一行混了很久的老前辈了。

568
00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:24.450
And I actually thought it was really cool.
其实我觉得这真的很酷。

569
00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:33.610
I'm still very humbled by it all that, you know, that I got to stop temping and got to, you know,
我依然对这一切感到非常谦卑，你知道，我终于不用再做临时工了，而是能够，你知道，

570
00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:40.630
take the skills I learned from my improv classes and get paid to do something I love.
运用我在即兴表演课上学到的技能，靠做我热爱的事情赚钱。

571
00:24:40.630 --> 00:24:47.890
It's still very humbling to me that people watch the show, enjoy the show, want to watch me be silly or whatever it is.
人们观看这部剧、喜欢这部剧、想看我耍宝或者做其他什么，这对我来说依然是一件让我感到谦卑的事情。

572
00:24:48.010 --> 00:24:50.090
You know, I always feel like I won the lottery.
你知道，我总觉得自己像中了彩票一样。

573
00:24:50.090 --> 00:24:52.530
So I'm still taken aback by it, like what you're saying.
所以我对你刚才说的那种感觉依然感到受宠若惊。

574
00:24:52.690 --> 00:24:54.630
It just I don't think that'll ever go away.
我觉得这种感觉永远不会消失。

575
00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:59.210
I'm still I still can't believe I I got to do this, you know.
我依然，我依然不敢相信我竟然能做到这一点，你知道。

576
00:24:59.710 --> 00:25:01.050
I wonder if Kathy Bates had that feeling.
我在想凯西·贝茨当时是不是也有这种感觉。

577
00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:06.630
Yeah, I will tell you, here's an example of when maybe it's not fun or appropriate.
是啊，我跟你说，这里有个例子，说明什么时候这种事可能既不有趣也不合时宜。

578
00:25:07.310 --> 00:25:12.090
I, towards the end of my pregnancy, was having some pain and they said, OK,
我在怀孕后期时感到有些疼痛，他们说，好吧，

579
00:25:12.190 --> 00:25:17.650
let's get you to the specialist for this special kind of sonogram where they can make sure everything's OK.
我们带你去见专家做一种特殊的超声波检查，确保一切正常。

580
00:25:17.930 --> 00:25:19.770
I was having a lot of pain in my pelvis.
当时我的骨盆非常疼。

581
00:25:20.250 --> 00:25:24.930
So I met with this person I've never met before and he's conducting the sonogram.
于是我见到了这位我从未谋面的医生，他正在给我做超声波检查。

582
00:25:25.270 --> 00:25:29.930
You know, I'm just like laying there and he's like, so is Dwight going to be stopping by?
你知道，我正躺在那儿，他突然问，那么德怀特会过来吗？

583
00:25:30.110 --> 00:25:30.870
Oh, boy.
天哪。

584
00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:31.330
Uh-huh.
嗯。

585
00:25:31.570 --> 00:25:37.370
And I was like, you mean a fictional character I'm in a television show with that is not the father of my baby?
我当时心想，你是说那个和我一起拍电视剧的虚构角色吗？他又不是我孩子的父亲？

586
00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:37.850
No.
不是。

587
00:25:38.990 --> 00:25:39.390
What?
什么？

588
00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:43.270
Like, I'm coming to you because I have pain in my pelvis.
我是因为骨盆疼才来找你看病的。

589
00:25:43.450 --> 00:25:44.010
That's so horrible.
那太糟糕了。

590
00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:48.930
And you're giving me a sonogram and I'm laying here feeling so exposed.
你正在给我做超声波检查，我躺在那儿感觉非常无助。

591
00:25:49.070 --> 00:25:53.070
And you asked me, I knew he was like, and he had been like 20 minutes.
而你竟然问我那个问题，我知道他憋了很久，大概憋了20分钟。

592
00:25:53.230 --> 00:25:54.970
So he'd been holding on to that.
所以他一直忍着没问。

593
00:25:55.070 --> 00:25:57.110
And then he was just like, when is Dwight stopping by?
然后他突然就问，德怀特什么时候过来？

594
00:25:57.270 --> 00:25:59.930
So maybe, maybe here's a tip.
所以也许，也许这里有个建议。

595
00:25:59.930 --> 00:26:04.670
Like, if you're in that situation, maybe don't bring up the TV show.
如果你处于那种情况，也许不要提起电视节目。

596
00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:11.610
I feel like medical professionals in particular should never bring up your celebrity in an exam room.
我觉得医疗专业人员尤其不应该在检查室里提起你的明星身份。

597
00:26:12.170 --> 00:26:13.430
Well, I think that's fair.
嗯，我觉得这很合理。

598
00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:14.950
I think it's fair to you.
我觉得这对你来说很公平。

599
00:26:14.950 --> 00:26:17.170
Just a good, solid boundary.
这确实是一个很好的、坚实的界限。

600
00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:18.250
Yeah.
是啊。

601
00:26:18.570 --> 00:26:21.150
You're probably there because you're worried about something.
你可能正因为担心某事而来到这里。

602
00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:21.490
Yes.
是的。

603
00:26:21.490 --> 00:26:21.990
You're concerned.
你很忧虑。

604
00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:22.250
Yes, that's right.
是的，没错。

605
00:26:22.590 --> 00:26:23.170
That's right.
确实如此。

606
00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:23.390
Yeah.
嗯。

607
00:26:23.670 --> 00:26:24.050
You know what?
你知道吗？

608
00:26:24.130 --> 00:26:24.990
Tell me in the lobby.
在大厅里告诉我吧。

609
00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:26.470
Tell me in the lobby afterwards.
事后在大厅里告诉我。

610
00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:27.270
You need to be you.
你需要做你自己。

611
00:26:27.490 --> 00:26:27.650
Yeah.
嗯。

612
00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:27.950
Right.
对。

613
00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:31.210
You need to be who you are and know that that person sees you.
你需要做真实的自己，并知道那个人能看见真正的你。

614
00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:31.590
Right.
对。

615
00:26:31.810 --> 00:26:40.930
You know, I think an analogy that I think of all the time is like, I'm an adult person and I pay a mortgage and I'm raising two children and, and all this stuff.
你知道，我经常会想到一个类比，就像我是一个成年人，要付房贷，还要抚养两个孩子，诸如此类的事情。

616
00:26:40.930 --> 00:26:50.750
And yet, all the time, I feel like a little bit of like, I can't believe that I am tasked with these adult responsibilities.
然而，我一直觉得，有点不敢相信自己竟然承担着这些成年人的责任。

617
00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:57.270
Like, I can't, like, young people will look at me and I know that I look like an old person to them.
就像，我无法……年轻人看着我时，我知道在他们眼里我看起来像个长辈。

618
00:26:57.390 --> 00:27:03.990
But like, inside, I still kind of feel like a flailing 23-year-old who doesn't know what they're doing.
但内心深处，我依然觉得自己像个手忙脚乱、不知所措的23岁年轻人。

619
00:27:04.410 --> 00:27:06.370
So I feel like that's part of it, too.
所以我感觉这也是其中的一部分。

620
00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:11.930
Like, when people are approaching me and they're like, oh, my God, I love your body of work and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.
比如，当人们走近我，说“天哪，我太喜欢你的作品了”之类的客套话时。

621
00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:17.250
I still feel like the struggling actor who's trying to make it.
我依然觉得自己是个还在努力打拼的演员。

622
00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:19.030
Like, that's never left me.
那种感觉从未离开过我。

623
00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:25.430
I will say, like, before I was on the show, that blog post you wrote to actors.
我想说，在我上节目之前，你写给演员的那篇博文……

624
00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:28.210
Yeah, that I turned into a book later.
对，后来我把它改成了书。

625
00:27:28.470 --> 00:27:28.530
Yeah.
是的。

626
00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:30.890
But at that point, I think it was just available.
但在那时，我想它只是公开发布的。

627
00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:31.910
It was just a blog poSt.
那只是一篇博文。

628
00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:32.280
Yeah.
嗯。

629
00:27:32.810 --> 00:27:33.750
Advice to Actors.
《给演员的建议》。

630
00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:38.650
It was so, because I'd read that a lot, multiple times before ever meeting you or being on the show.
那真的……因为在见到你或上节目之前，我读过很多次。

631
00:27:38.650 --> 00:27:40.750
Oh, my gosh, I didn't even know that, Zach.
天哪，Zach，我甚至都不知道这一点。

632
00:27:40.990 --> 00:27:42.630
Yeah, it was so orienting.
是的，那真的很有指引作用。

633
00:27:42.770 --> 00:27:51.590
I think, because a lot of times I think when people can feel the distance between how they're perceived and how, and their interior sense of themselves, what you're describing.
我想，因为很多时候，当人们感受到外界对他们的看法与他们内心对自己的认知之间存在差距时，也就是你所描述的那种情况。

634
00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:59.730
I think sometimes people's response to that is to try to identify publicly only with the way they're perceived or something.
我觉得有时人们的反应是试图在公开场合只认同那种被外界感知的形象，或者类似的事情。

635
00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:16.290
But I remember for you at that time to share the kind of blemishy, angsty, unsure kind of experience that you'd had was so helpful because it's so isolating to aspire to something.
但我记得当时你分享了自己那些不完美、焦虑、不确定的经历，这非常有帮助，因为怀揣梦想时往往会感到非常孤独。

636
00:28:16.290 --> 00:28:17.730
Well, I don't know if that's even true.
嗯，我不知道那是否属实。

637
00:28:18.390 --> 00:28:20.650
It's scary to, what am I trying to say?
这很可怕，我想表达什么来着？

638
00:28:20.990 --> 00:28:23.930
There's a Cherry Jones, you know, Cherry Jones, the actor, Cherry Jones.
有一位演员叫切莉·琼斯，你知道切莉·琼斯吧。

639
00:28:24.430 --> 00:28:29.070
She has this quote that I love so much where she says, theaters where we comfort each other with our shortcomings,
她有句我很喜欢的话，她说：“剧院是我们用彼此的缺点来慰藉对方的地方。”

640
00:28:29.350 --> 00:28:34.310
whereas I'll show you mine and you'll see yours reflected and then we'll have a night of laughs and catharsis and whatever.
我会展示我的缺点，你也会看到自己的缺点在其中映照，然后我们会一起度过一个充满欢笑和宣泄的夜晚，诸如此类。

641
00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:38.650
In a way, like when you wrote that thing, it's like, not that that shortcomings exactly,
某种程度上，当你写那篇文章时，虽然那不完全算是缺点，

642
00:28:38.790 --> 00:28:44.510
but I felt comforted by your vulnerability and by your ownership of that vulnerability because I felt so vulnerable.
但我从你的脆弱以及你对那种脆弱的坦诚中得到了安慰，因为我也感到非常脆弱。

643
00:28:45.030 --> 00:28:51.350
So seeing like, oh, wow, someone who I admire, who has a, like a exciting career and who, you know,
所以看到像你这样我钦佩的人，拥有令人兴奋的职业生涯，而且……

644
00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:59.510
is willing to be introspective and revealing in this way and expose the gap between public perception and interior experience.
愿意以这种方式进行内省和剖析，揭示公众认知与内心体验之间的鸿沟。

645
00:28:59.510 --> 00:29:02.170
Like that for me was really helpful.
对我来说，这真的很有帮助。

646
00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:04.410
Sorry, that's such a long-winded answer.
抱歉，我的回答太冗长了。

647
00:29:04.510 --> 00:29:05.550
I loved every word of that.
我喜欢你说的每一个字。

648
00:29:05.870 --> 00:29:11.690
No, and I think it's your question too, which is like, we were so in awe of Kathy Bates, but like,
不，而且我觉得这也回应了你的问题，就像我们对凯西·贝茨充满敬畏，但……

649
00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:20.850
maybe Kathy Bates just feels like the same tripping over her own feet actor that we all feel like when we first start a role or first start a job or whatever.
也许凯西·贝茨也觉得自己和我们刚开始一个角色或一份工作时一样，是个会笨手笨脚的演员。

650
00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:26.510
But we never asked because we just assumed she must be filled with all this confidence and all of this, all of it.
但我们从未问过，因为我们理所当然地认为她一定充满自信，拥有这一切。

651
00:29:26.510 --> 00:29:32.870
But that also goes back to you, Zach, you talk about all this anxiety and you're holding these things in Walmart and you can't believe you're on the show.
但这也要归功于你自己，扎克，你谈到那些焦虑，说你在沃尔玛拿着这些东西时，简直不敢相信自己竟然上了这个节目。

652
00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:38.510
You were seamlessly a part of our show from the very beginning.
你从一开始就完美地融入了我们的节目。

653
00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:48.790
Like, it's kind of blowing my mind that you're telling me that you were anxious or that you even were inexperienced because I knew you had done In the Loop,
说实话，你告诉我你当时很焦虑，甚至没什么经验，这让我感到非常震惊，因为我知道你演过《灵通人士》，

654
00:29:48.990 --> 00:29:51.430
but I assumed like there were all these other things too.
但我以为你肯定还演过其他很多作品。

655
00:29:51.430 --> 00:30:00.370
I had no, I really had no idea that The Office was your first television job, like, and for how just amazing you were, right?
我真的完全没想到《办公室》是你第一份电视工作，毕竟你表现得那么出色，对吧？

656
00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:04.430
So just amazing right out of the gate and such a smart improviser.
一上来就表现得如此惊艳，而且是个极其聪明的即兴表演者。

657
00:30:04.530 --> 00:30:10.550
I know I'm going to be an improv nerd here, but I just love it when people are really smart and say really witty,
我知道我又要表现得像个即兴表演书呆子了，但我就是喜欢那些非常聪明的人，他们能说出非常机智、

658
00:30:10.550 --> 00:30:16.330
smart things and don't go for the obvious joke and don't go for too much and are understated and you're all of those things.
聪明的话，不会去刻意追求显而易见的笑点，也不会用力过猛，而是表现得恰到好处，而你完全做到了这些。

659
00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:25.610
And I have loved watching your scenes so much and I love going to the script and reading the script and then watching your scenes because you,
我非常喜欢看你的表演，也喜欢先读剧本，然后再看你的戏，因为我觉得，

660
00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:29.530
I think maybe you and Steve improvised the moSt.
可能你和史蒂夫是即兴表演最多的。

661
00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:35.230
Honestly, if you look at how many episodes you're in and improvise so well, you know?
说真的，看看你参演的那些集数，你的即兴表演真的太棒了，你知道吗？

662
00:30:35.750 --> 00:30:36.750
That's very sweet.
你太客气了。

663
00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:38.910
I mean, I, yeah, it's so nice.
我是说，是的，这真是太好了。

664
00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:50.610
I started taking acting classes because I, I would hire in between, like when we'd be shooting, I would go back to New York and I had this acting coach named Anya Saffer,
我开始上表演课是因为，在拍摄间隙，我会回到纽约，找一位叫安雅·萨弗的表演教练，

665
00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:55.330
who is this, who I would go, shout out to Anya Saffer, because I'd never gone to acting school or anything.
我要向安雅·萨弗致敬，因为我从来没上过表演学校之类的。

666
00:30:55.330 --> 00:31:02.970
So I would go through like scenes from Tennessee Williams Place and like take voice classes and do like Alexander Technique and all this shit because I was like, I just felt,
所以我当时会去排练田纳西·威廉斯剧作里的片段，上发声课，练习亚历山大技巧之类的，因为我当时觉得，

667
00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:06.610
I had such deep imposter syndrome because I hadn't done it before.
我患有严重的冒名顶替综合征，因为我之前没做过这些。

668
00:31:06.710 --> 00:31:10.650
So it's a, it's nice to hear that it wasn't, wasn't flagrantly obvious.
所以听到你说这并不明显，我感到很欣慰。

669
00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:11.150
No.
不明显。

670
00:31:11.410 --> 00:31:11.870
That's good.
那很好。

671
00:31:12.190 --> 00:31:12.890
That's a relief.
这让我松了一口气。

672
00:31:12.910 --> 00:31:18.450
This is a great segue for us to ask you if you had ever played Abe Lincoln other than on The Office.
这正好引出一个话题，想问问你除了在《办公室》里，你还演过亚伯拉罕·林肯吗？

673
00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:26.470
I'd been sent an audition for Abe Lincoln Vampire Hunter where I had to learn the Gettysburg Address and I was not hired to be Abe Lincoln Vampire Hunter.
我曾收到过《吸血鬼猎人林肯》的试镜邀请，当时我必须背下《葛底斯堡演说》，但我最终没被选上出演《吸血鬼猎人林肯》。

674
00:31:26.910 --> 00:31:29.130
But other than that, I'd never played Abe Lincoln.
除此之外，我从未演过林肯。

675
00:31:29.390 --> 00:31:35.970
Wait, so Abe Lincoln recited the Gettysburg Address while killing vampires or being a vampire.
等等，所以林肯是在杀吸血鬼或者变成吸血鬼的时候背诵《葛底斯堡演说》吗？

676
00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:37.730
Vampire Hunter.
是《吸血鬼猎人》。

677
00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:39.050
I think he hunted the vampires.
我想他是去猎杀吸血鬼的。

678
00:31:39.390 --> 00:31:40.090
Oh, he hunted the vampires.
哦，他是去猎杀吸血鬼的。

679
00:31:40.270 --> 00:31:41.290
But you know how it is with vampires.
但你知道吸血鬼是怎么回事。

680
00:31:41.330 --> 00:31:42.590
You start out hunting them and then.
你一开始是去猎杀他们，然后……

681
00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:43.550
You want to be one.
你就想成为他们中的一员。

682
00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:44.170
You become one.
你就变成了他们。

683
00:31:44.270 --> 00:31:44.490
Yeah.
没错。

684
00:31:44.490 --> 00:31:54.230
I think they just wanted to see people do the Gettysburg Address to see if they were plausible as Lincoln and then they were going to like get to the vampire hunting in like a later stage of the casting process or something.
我想他们只是想看看人们背诵《葛底斯堡演说》的样子，以此判断他们是否适合演林肯，然后再在选角的后期阶段去处理吸血鬼猎杀的部分之类的。

685
00:31:54.910 --> 00:31:57.810
So I did for a while know the Gettysburg Address.
所以我确实有一段时间会背《葛底斯堡演说》。

686
00:31:57.990 --> 00:32:01.090
Because you had done that prior to playing Abe Lincoln on The Office?
因为你在《办公室》里演林肯之前就做过这些？

687
00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:02.010
Yeah, I think so.
是的，我想是这样。

688
00:32:02.130 --> 00:32:02.570
I see.
我明白了。

689
00:32:02.790 --> 00:32:03.130
Wow.
哇。

690
00:32:03.310 --> 00:32:03.470
Yeah.
是的。

691
00:32:03.670 --> 00:32:09.210
So there was a small part of you that had played Abe Lincoln if not for an audition.
所以你身上确实有一小部分演过林肯，哪怕只是为了试镜。

692
00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:12.210
I mean like my body had played Abe Lincoln my entire life.
我是说，我的身体这辈子都在演林肯。

693
00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:19.730
If I had the doctor, I remember Abe Lincoln had something called Marfan syndrome, which is like this, I guess it's like a congenital heart defect or something.
我记得医生说过，林肯患有一种叫马凡氏综合征的病，这好像是一种先天性心脏缺陷之类的。

694
00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:20.150
I don't know.
我也不太清楚。

695
00:32:20.290 --> 00:32:27.450
Basically, like the symptoms are your wingspan is greater than your height and you have something called a pectus excavatum, I think, which I have.
基本上，症状就是你的臂展超过身高，而且还有一种叫漏斗胸的东西，我想我就是这样。

696
00:32:27.570 --> 00:32:29.090
It's like this weird divot in your cheSt.
就是胸口有个奇怪的凹陷。

697
00:32:29.270 --> 00:32:33.450
I remember going to the doctor and him being like, oh, Abe Lincoln had Marfan syndrome.
我记得去看医生时，他说，哦，林肯患有马凡氏综合征。

698
00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:34.530
We're going to screen you for it.
我们要给你做个筛查。

699
00:32:34.530 --> 00:32:43.410
And then being just shocked that I didn't have it because I had like all the symptoms of Abe Lincoln's disease, but not the actual diagnosis.
结果发现我没有，当时还挺震惊的，因为我身上有林肯那种病的所有症状，但并没有确诊。

700
00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:44.670
Not to brag, hold your applause.
不是在吹牛，请先别鼓掌。

701
00:32:47.710 --> 00:32:51.810
What are some of your favorite scenes or moments from the show?
你觉得这部剧里有哪些你最喜欢的场景或时刻？

702
00:32:52.430 --> 00:32:58.370
It's funny, like I remember hearing some interview with Mark Rylance, the actor, he said like when you die,
很有趣，我记得听过演员马克·里朗斯的一次采访，他说当你去世时，

703
00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:04.290
probably like the thing that flashes before your eyes isn't the episodes of the shows, but the experience you had making them.
在你眼前闪过的可能不是剧集本身，而是你制作它们时的经历。

704
00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:11.810
Like, and I think for me, when I think of the show, it's sort of a gauzy feeling of like, I remember lunch a lot.
对我来说，当我想到这部剧时，那是一种朦胧的感觉，比如我经常想起午餐时间。

705
00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:16.050
I remember like hanging out with people like in between setups and stuff.
我记得在布景间隙和大家闲聊之类的时光。

706
00:33:16.250 --> 00:33:26.470
In terms of scenes, like I think it took me a while till after I was off the office when I started to be able to really enjoy scenes because I didn't feel like, oh God,
至于场景，我觉得直到我离开《办公室》一段时间后，我才真正开始享受那些场景，因为我不再觉得，天哪，

707
00:33:26.550 --> 00:33:27.730
what if I f*** this up, you know?
万一我搞砸了怎么办，你知道吗？

708
00:33:27.910 --> 00:33:28.310
Right, right.
没错，没错。

709
00:33:28.430 --> 00:33:30.390
You could be an audience member and watch it.
你可以作为一个观众去欣赏它。

710
00:33:30.550 --> 00:33:30.850
Yeah.
是啊。

711
00:33:30.850 --> 00:33:35.910
And I really liked stuff where I liked getting into fights with Ed HelMs.
我真的很喜欢和艾德·赫尔姆斯吵架的那些戏份。

712
00:33:36.030 --> 00:33:36.770
I thought that was fun.
我觉得那很有趣。

713
00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:38.130
I like menacing Ed HelMs.
我喜欢威胁艾德·赫尔姆斯。

714
00:33:38.530 --> 00:33:42.790
I looked online to see what are the fan favorite quotes of Gabe.
我在网上查了一下粉丝最喜欢的盖博语录。

715
00:33:42.970 --> 00:33:43.410
Oh, really?
哦，真的吗？

716
00:33:43.810 --> 00:33:44.090
Yeah.
是的。

717
00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:45.570
And I share one of them.
我想分享其中一条。

718
00:33:45.670 --> 00:33:46.010
Please.
请讲。

719
00:33:46.230 --> 00:33:46.950
With the internet.
分享给互联网。

720
00:33:47.330 --> 00:33:59.910
So I love so much in search committee when you just start asking him questions about the sun and then Andy knows all this information about the sun and you say shut up about the sun.
我非常喜欢在《搜寻委员会》那一集里，你开始问他关于太阳的问题，然后安迪对太阳了如指掌，结果你说“闭嘴，别再提太阳了”。

721
00:33:59.910 --> 00:34:01.370
Shut up about the sun.
闭嘴，别再提太阳了。

722
00:34:01.670 --> 00:34:04.430
So that made it as one of best Gabe quotes.
所以那成了盖博最棒的语录之一。

723
00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:05.590
It's also one of mine.
那也是我最喜欢的语录之一。

724
00:34:06.150 --> 00:34:07.150
Here's another one.
还有这一句。

725
00:34:07.250 --> 00:34:08.290
Walk away, bitch.
走开，贱人。

726
00:34:08.890 --> 00:34:10.030
Oh, yeah.
哦，对。

727
00:34:10.190 --> 00:34:11.030
That was to Andy, right?
那是对安迪说的，对吧？

728
00:34:11.090 --> 00:34:11.730
Yeah, that's to Andy.
是的，那是对安迪说的。

729
00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:12.290
I remember that.
我记得那一幕。

730
00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:16.630
And then this is one I forgot and I actually had to go and rewatch the episode.
还有这一句我忘了，我甚至不得不回去重看那一集。

731
00:34:16.790 --> 00:34:18.070
We haven't gotten to it yet, Jenna.
我们还没讲到那一集呢，珍娜。

732
00:34:18.070 --> 00:34:19.510
It's from Turf War.
那是《地盘之争》那一集。

733
00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:25.590
And Gabe says, sometimes I wonder if I have ovaries in my scrotum because I am great at girl talk.
盖博说：“有时候我怀疑我的阴囊里是不是有卵巢，因为我太擅长女生之间的谈话了。”

734
00:34:25.870 --> 00:34:26.630
Jesus ChriSt.
天哪。

735
00:34:27.230 --> 00:34:28.490
That's the most upsetting.
那太让人反胃了。

736
00:34:28.650 --> 00:34:28.990
Sorry.
抱歉。

737
00:34:29.230 --> 00:34:30.050
Sorry, America.
抱歉，美国观众。

738
00:34:31.390 --> 00:34:34.330
That's the most upsetting phrase I've ever heard.
那是我听过最让人不适的话了。

739
00:34:34.450 --> 00:34:35.010
Oh, my gosh.
天呐。

740
00:34:35.130 --> 00:34:38.530
And the other one was, there are plenty of people who love touching me.
还有另一句是：“有很多人喜欢触碰我。”

741
00:34:38.890 --> 00:34:41.170
Oh, I remember that scene.
哦，我记得那个场景。

742
00:34:41.450 --> 00:34:41.830
That's heartbreaking.
那太令人心碎了。

743
00:34:41.830 --> 00:34:43.390
It's when Kelly hugs Gabe.
那是凯莉拥抱盖博的时候。

744
00:34:54.940 --> 00:35:00.820
When we were rewatching Search Committee, it's Toby, Gabe, and Jim are the Search Committee.
当我们重看《搜寻委员会》时，托比、盖博和吉姆是搜寻委员会成员。

745
00:35:00.820 --> 00:35:05.700
And you got to interview all of those amazing actors doing the cameos.
你们采访了所有那些客串的优秀演员。

746
00:35:05.880 --> 00:35:07.340
Are there any that stood out to you?
有没有哪位让你印象深刻？

747
00:35:07.820 --> 00:35:12.760
I remember Will Arnett would do this thing where he would sort of start improvising.
我记得威尔·阿奈特会做一些即兴发挥。

748
00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:18.400
He'd be joking around in this way that was so unbelievably funny in between takes.
他在拍摄间隙开玩笑的方式简直好笑得不可思议。

749
00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:21.880
And then when they would call action, it was sort of like just continue in a way.
然后当导演喊“开始”时，他就像是顺着刚才的状态继续演下去。

750
00:35:21.880 --> 00:35:23.960
I remember being like, oh, wow, that's fascinating.
我记得当时心想，哇，那太迷人了。

751
00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:28.320
Like he's kind of in a, it doesn't stop in between cut and action.
就像他一直处于某种状态，在喊停和开拍之间都不会中断。

752
00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:30.380
He's like kind of, not in a tiring way.
他那种状态，倒不是说让人觉得累。

753
00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:32.060
You know, there's people who are like on in a way.
你知道，有些人就是那种随时随地都在状态里的人。

754
00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:32.700
It wasn't like that.
他不是那样的。

755
00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:38.160
It was just like he was sort of in this, it was like he was keeping it like simmering or something.
他就像是处于某种状态中，感觉他一直让那种情绪保持着，或者类似的情况。

756
00:35:38.300 --> 00:35:40.240
I remember being fascinated by that.
我记得当时觉得特别着迷。

757
00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:40.580
Yeah.
是啊。

758
00:35:40.780 --> 00:35:43.160
And I remember feeling like immediately like Ray Romano.
我还记得当时立刻就对雷·罗马诺有了那种感觉。

759
00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:45.100
I just went to like rest my head on his shoulder.
我当时就想把头靠在他的肩膀上。

760
00:35:45.300 --> 00:35:47.860
He was like so warm and sweet.
他真的特别温暖、特别贴心。

761
00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:49.780
And I really loved him.
我真的很喜欢他。

762
00:35:49.780 --> 00:35:52.340
And then I also like, it was fun.
而且我也觉得，那很有趣。

763
00:35:52.400 --> 00:35:56.620
I remember we like interviewed people from the office, which was fun to do.
我记得我们采访了《办公室》里的人，那过程很有意思。

764
00:35:56.820 --> 00:36:00.400
Like your interview of Mindy as Kelly was really funny.
比如你采访明迪饰演的凯利那段就特别好笑。

765
00:36:00.400 --> 00:36:07.340
And I think it's in the bloopers because Gabe decides that, oh, maybe I'm going to be the voice of reason here.
我记得那段应该在花絮里，因为盖博决定，哦，也许我该在这里当个理智的人。

766
00:36:07.480 --> 00:36:12.560
And he looks at Jim and Toby when Kelly walks in and goes, we don't really need to go through all this, do we?
当凯利走进来时，他看着吉姆和托比说，我们没必要非得走完这个流程吧？

767
00:36:12.740 --> 00:36:13.620
And we're not, we're not.
我们没必要，真的没必要。

768
00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:15.780
This isn't like a serious interview, right?
这不是什么严肃的面试，对吧？

769
00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:18.200
And they both just throw you under the bus.
结果他们俩直接把你给卖了。

770
00:36:18.400 --> 00:36:20.660
And Toby's like, no, I think we, it is serious.
托比说，不，我觉得我们，这确实很严肃。

771
00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.680
And then Gabe says to Kelly, what are your weaknesses?
然后盖博问凯利，你有什么缺点吗？

772
00:36:24.020 --> 00:36:26.160
And Kelly goes, I don't have any assholes.
凯利回答说，我没有任何混蛋的地方。

773
00:36:27.380 --> 00:36:28.340
Something like that.
差不多是这样。

774
00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:34.560
But you guys, you could tell that Mindy was like breaking, you know, and just the whole scene is so funny.
但你们能看出来明迪当时快憋不住笑了，你知道的，整场戏真的太搞笑了。

775
00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:37.080
I imagine there's a lot of great bloopers from that day.
我猜那天肯定有很多精彩的花絮。

776
00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:45.920
Yeah, I mean, and by that point, I guess that was late enough in the shooting process where I felt like my blood pressure had dropped incrementally a little bit and I was able to.
是啊，而且到那个阶段，我觉得拍摄已经进行得够久了，我的血压稍微降下来了一点，我也能……

777
00:36:46.040 --> 00:36:47.340
It looked like you were having fun.
看起来你当时玩得很开心。

778
00:36:47.380 --> 00:36:56.880
I remember enjoying those scenes more where I was like, okay, like, I think after they brought me back for another season, I thought like, well, if they were horrified,
我记得我更享受那些场景了，当时我想，好吧，我觉得在他们让我回归下一季之后，我想，如果他们觉得很糟糕的话，

779
00:36:56.920 --> 00:36:57.880
they wouldn't have done that.
他们就不会这么做了。

780
00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:04.800
And that really put me at ease where I just thought, okay, well, if they've decided they want me here, then it's their funeral.
这让我感到很安心，我心想，好吧，既然他们决定让我留在这里，那后果就由他们承担吧。

781
00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:08.160
So I can't, you know, I'll take it.
所以我没法拒绝，你知道，我会接受的。

782
00:37:08.380 --> 00:37:15.140
The other thing, when I looked up the character Gabe and I did like an image search, one of the first things that comes up is Gabe is Lady Gaga.
还有件事，当我搜索盖博这个角色，做图片搜索时，跳出来的第一张图就是盖博扮成Lady Gaga的样子。

783
00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:15.940
Yeah.
是啊。

784
00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:19.140
That costume was, oh my gosh.
那套服装，天哪。

785
00:37:19.160 --> 00:37:22.400
I just remember you were in hair and makeup for so long.
我只记得你做发型和化妆花了特别久的时间。

786
00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:22.780
Yeah.
是啊。

787
00:37:23.020 --> 00:37:24.060
You had the eyelashes.
你还戴了假睫毛。

788
00:37:24.460 --> 00:37:24.620
Yeah, yeah.
对，没错。

789
00:37:24.940 --> 00:37:25.200
Yeah.
是啊。

790
00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:28.620
I've never felt more like myself on set.
我从来没在片场感觉自己这么像自己过。

791
00:37:28.820 --> 00:37:31.540
I think that, you know, I never walked in high heels before.
我想，你知道，我以前从没穿过高跟鞋。

792
00:37:31.760 --> 00:37:33.660
I don't know where they found high heels my size.
我不知道他们从哪找来我这个尺码的高跟鞋。

793
00:37:33.660 --> 00:37:34.760
What's your shoe size?
你穿多大码的鞋？

794
00:37:34.940 --> 00:37:37.100
11 and a half, a modest 11 and a half.
11码半，谦虚的11码半。

795
00:37:37.180 --> 00:37:43.680
We're not, you know, but it was, I don't know if you guys know this, extremely hard to walk in.
我们不是，你知道，但这真的，我不知道你们知不知道，穿那个走路太难了。

796
00:37:44.840 --> 00:37:46.000
We have an idea.
我们大概能体会。

797
00:37:46.220 --> 00:37:46.380
What?
什么？

798
00:37:46.660 --> 00:37:47.080
Yeah, yeah.
是啊，是啊。

799
00:37:47.300 --> 00:37:47.920
I know.
我知道。

800
00:37:48.040 --> 00:37:49.980
No man has ever told you this.
从来没有男人跟你说过这个吧。

801
00:37:51.420 --> 00:37:53.280
It was so hard to move.
当时真的很难动弹。

802
00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:54.080
I couldn't believe it.
我简直不敢相信。

803
00:37:54.160 --> 00:37:56.640
I was like, wait, people do this for hours at a time?
我当时心想，等等，人们竟然能穿着这玩意儿一站就是好几个小时？

804
00:37:56.820 --> 00:37:57.800
It's kind of insane.
这简直太疯狂了。

805
00:37:58.000 --> 00:37:59.760
It's ridiculous when you think about it.
仔细想想，这真的很荒谬。

806
00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:02.360
It's like, I'm going to design a shoe where you're up on your tippy toes.
就像是有人说，我要设计一款让你踮着脚尖走路的鞋子。

807
00:38:02.360 --> 00:38:03.480
Now go walk all day.
然后让你穿着它走上一整天。

808
00:38:03.740 --> 00:38:04.520
Not just that.
不仅如此。

809
00:38:04.520 --> 00:38:05.680
So many high heels.
还有好多高跟鞋。

810
00:38:05.900 --> 00:38:09.480
They're made for a foot where you just have one long center toe.
它们的设计根本就是给那种只有一根长长中趾的脚穿的。

811
00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:10.660
Yeah, it's true.
没错，确实如此。

812
00:38:10.660 --> 00:38:12.560
It's like not even the shape of your foot.
那鞋型甚至根本就不是脚的形状。

813
00:38:12.760 --> 00:38:13.480
Which luckily I do have.
幸运的是，我正好有那种脚趾。

814
00:38:13.580 --> 00:38:13.980
That is all.
就是这样。

815
00:38:13.980 --> 00:38:15.380
I just have a long center toe.
我正好有一根长长的中趾。

816
00:38:15.380 --> 00:38:16.900
That was also an Abraham Lincoln.
那也是亚伯拉罕·林肯的特征之一。

817
00:38:17.120 --> 00:38:19.500
Yes, that's one of the symptoms of Marfan's syndrome.
是的，那是马凡氏综合征的症状之一。

818
00:38:19.880 --> 00:38:21.600
Just a single long toe.
就是只有一根长脚趾。

819
00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:27.600
In terms of volume, it's the same volume as five toes, but just in length.
从体积上来说，它和五根脚趾的体积是一样的，只是长度不同。

820
00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:32.380
All right, we have one other question we've been curious about.
好了，我们还有一个一直很好奇的问题。

821
00:38:32.600 --> 00:38:34.040
The Great Danes.
关于大丹犬。

822
00:38:34.260 --> 00:38:36.760
You had to work a lot with these giant dogs.
你得和这些巨大的狗狗一起工作。

823
00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:40.740
Was there anything, like any memory from working with those dogs?
有什么特别的吗？比如和那些狗一起工作时有什么记忆？

824
00:38:41.080 --> 00:38:43.420
Yeah, they were like frustratingly professional.
是啊，它们专业得让人沮丧。

825
00:38:43.780 --> 00:38:44.000
Really?
真的吗？

826
00:38:44.040 --> 00:38:44.440
Yeah.
是的。

827
00:38:44.660 --> 00:38:48.320
I was like, you know, with a dog, you just want to like rub your face in their snout.
我当时心想，你知道的，面对一只狗，你只想把脸埋进它们的鼻子里蹭蹭。

828
00:38:48.460 --> 00:38:49.940
You just want to be like, oh, I love you.
你只想对它们说，噢，我爱你。

829
00:38:49.980 --> 00:38:50.540
You're beautiful.
你真漂亮。

830
00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:51.200
You're a miracle.
你简直是个奇迹。

831
00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:53.280
And they were just like, we're working.
但它们却表现得像是在说，我们在工作呢。

832
00:38:53.620 --> 00:38:55.320
They just were so focused.
它们真的太专注了。

833
00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:59.060
They had that like working dog thing where they were just like not screwing around.
它们身上有一种工作犬的特质，就是那种绝不胡闹的态度。

834
00:38:59.380 --> 00:38:59.500
Yeah.
是啊。

835
00:38:59.760 --> 00:39:00.100
And I was like.
我当时就觉得。

836
00:39:00.220 --> 00:39:00.860
No snuggles.
不给抱抱。

837
00:39:01.020 --> 00:39:01.660
No snuggles.
不给抱抱。

838
00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:03.820
I was like, spoon me, Great Danes.
我当时心想，来抱抱我吧，大丹犬们。

839
00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:05.300
And they're like, no, thank you.
而它们却说，不用了，谢谢。

840
00:39:05.860 --> 00:39:07.440
No, thank you, strange human.
不用了，谢谢，奇怪的人类。

841
00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:09.180
I'm working.
我在工作呢。

842
00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:09.700
Yeah.
是啊。

843
00:39:09.900 --> 00:39:13.980
I was like, it truly was like, they were like, like, you ever meet like one of those former
我当时觉得，它们真的就像是，就像你有没有见过那种童星，

844
00:39:13.980 --> 00:39:16.780
child stars who's been like working from the time they were three and they're like,
从三岁起就开始工作，然后表现得像是在说，

845
00:39:16.820 --> 00:39:17.300
where's my light?
我的灯光在哪儿？

846
00:39:17.500 --> 00:39:17.840
Okay.
好了。

847
00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:20.040
I just need a little bit of eyebrow pencil and let's go.
我只需要一点眉笔，我们开始吧。

848
00:39:20.300 --> 00:39:24.200
You know, those people who are just like, they were like that, but just the canine version.
你知道那种人，它们就是那样，只不过是犬类版本。

849
00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:24.510
Yeah.
是啊。

850
00:39:24.900 --> 00:39:26.900
Do you share Gabe's love of horror films?
你和盖比一样喜欢恐怖片吗？

851
00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:27.760
No.
不。

852
00:39:28.060 --> 00:39:30.200
I mean, I like some horror movies, but not really.
我是说，我喜欢看一些恐怖片，但其实也没那么喜欢。

853
00:39:30.340 --> 00:39:33.440
What I do love is I really like wake up pranks.
我真正喜欢的是那种恶搞叫醒视频。

854
00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:34.860
Do you guys know about wake up pranks?
你们知道恶搞叫醒吗？

855
00:39:35.200 --> 00:39:35.560
No.
不知道。

856
00:39:35.820 --> 00:39:37.360
Do you wake up someone in a horrible manner?
就是那种用很可怕的方式把人叫醒？

857
00:39:37.520 --> 00:39:37.680
Yeah.
对。

858
00:39:37.720 --> 00:39:39.680
It's like people waking up their friends in mean ways.
就是人们用很坏的方式把朋友叫醒。

859
00:39:39.860 --> 00:39:40.180
Oh, no.
噢，不。

860
00:39:40.180 --> 00:39:41.140
And people get scared.
然后人们会被吓到。

861
00:39:41.140 --> 00:39:43.360
And I think it's very funny and very mean.
我觉得这既好笑又很坏。

862
00:39:43.580 --> 00:39:46.500
And there's one where they're like, this guy's asleep.
有一个视频是这样的，有个家伙睡着了。

863
00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:47.740
I guess they got like fast food.
我猜他们买了快餐。

864
00:39:47.860 --> 00:39:48.480
They're in a van.
他们在车里。

865
00:39:48.620 --> 00:39:49.240
This guy's asleep.
这家伙睡着了。

866
00:39:49.260 --> 00:39:52.700
He's got like a burger on his lap, but he fell asleep before he ate his burger.
他腿上放着个汉堡，但在吃之前就睡着了。

867
00:39:52.700 --> 00:39:56.740
And I think there's like a truck with one of those.
然后我觉得旁边有辆卡车之类的。

868
00:39:57.100 --> 00:39:59.820
It's like one of those trucks that pulls cars behind it.
就是那种后面拖着车的拖车。

869
00:39:59.860 --> 00:40:01.920
So, it looks like the car is facing.
所以，看起来那辆车正对着他们。

870
00:40:02.300 --> 00:40:02.840
Oh, yeah.
噢，对。

871
00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:03.740
Like the vehicle.
就像那辆车一样。

872
00:40:03.740 --> 00:40:06.420
So, you could mistake it as an oncoming vehicle.
所以，你会误以为那是迎面驶来的车。

873
00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:06.940
Right, right.
没错，没错。

874
00:40:07.400 --> 00:40:08.400
So, this guy's asleep.
所以，这家伙睡着了。

